            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 77,
            "end": 77,
            "text": "# before any action is emitted (no partial plans)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 83,
            "end": 90,
            "text": "Validate the caller-supplied release journal and return applied ids.\n\n    ``applied`` defaults to empty (absent or explicit null). A non-list value, a\n    non-string entry, or an empty-string entry is malformed (``invalid-change``).\n    Duplicates collapse to a set because the journal records facts, not\n    requests; whether those ids name known changes is a graph question, answered\n    by planner.py with ``unknown-dependency``."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 104,
            "end": 121,
            "text": "Validate a rollback retain list and return it as a set of applied ids.\n\n    ``retain`` defaults to empty (absent or explicit null). A non-list value, a\n    non-string entry, an empty-string entry, or an id that the journal does not\n    list as applied is a request contradiction -- nothing can be \"kept\" that was\n    never released -- so it fails with ``invalid-retain``.\n\n    Cross-module boundary: the *membership* test needs the applied set, which\n    belongs to the journal layer, but it deliberately stops there. Whether a\n    retained id names a real change, and which dependencies the closure pulls\n    in, are graph questions owned by planner.py (``unknown-dependency`` /\n    ``dependency-cycle``). Keeping the split means this function never needs the\n    graph and the caller cannot accidentally emit an action from here.\n\n    Duplicates collapse because retention is a set of facts, and the input must\n    be rejected before planner.py computes any action, so a rejected retain list\n    can never yield partial actions."
          }
        ],
        "planner.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 10,
            "text": "Compose policy and journal data into a plan.\n\nImport direction: planner.py -> journal.py, policy.py (unchanged). Nothing\nimports planner.py except the release.py entrypoint.\n\nResponsibilities: build the dependency graph, detect bad targets/cycles,\nproduce the deterministic topological order, apply replay, budget and rollback\nretention rules, and assemble the exact four-field result. No file, network or\ndeployment side effects: the plan is advisory only and never executes a release."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 25,
            "end": 31,
            "text": "Return (units_by_id, deps_by_id) after validating ids against the journal.\n\n    Every dependency and every persisted applied id must name a change in\n    ``changes``; otherwise the request is internally inconsistent and fails with\n    ``unknown-dependency``. This is the only place that decides id existence, so\n    journal.py can stay purely structural."
          },
