        "policy.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 7,
            "text": "Validate release policy before computing actions.\n\nImport direction: this module imports nothing from the other three modules; it\nis the environment/budget layer that planner.py consumes. It decides *whether*\nan operation is allowed and returns the validated request fields; it never\nbuilds graphs or emits actions."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 12,
            "end": 12,
            "text": "# G2 staging plan quota in units. The effective cap is min(request budget, this),"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 13,
            "end": 13,
            "text": "# so a larger request budget can never raise the limit above this value."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 22,
            "end": 42,
            "text": "Validate environment, action and budget; return (environment, action, budget).\n\n    Checks run in a fixed order (environment, action, budget) so the reported\n    error code is deterministic when several fields are wrong.\n\n    G2 freeze (acc-v3): ``environment == \"production\"`` raises\n    ``production-frozen`` as the very first decision. This ordering is the\n    requirement, not an optimisation: the freeze must win even over a malformed\n    ``changes``/``depends_on`` graph, so it cannot be delegated to a later\n    layer. It also covers both ``plan`` and ``rollback``, and it runs before the\n    action/budget checks on purpose -- a production request is frozen\n    regardless of which operation it names. The condition for this shortcut to\n    hold is that the caller reached the domain layer at all; release.py maps a\n    stdin parse failure to ``invalid-json`` before this function is called.\n\n    ``type(budget) is not int`` deliberately excludes bool, which subclasses\n    int. The budget value is returned but *not* enforced here: it is the\n    requested budget, and the effective cap differs by operation (staging plan\n    min(budget, PLAN_QUOTA); rollback uncapped), so the comparison against\n    emitted units belongs to planner.py, which knows the emitted actions."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 57,
            "end": 62,
            "text": "Return the budget cap actually applied, or None when uncapped.\n\n    Staging plan is capped at ``PLAN_QUOTA`` units however much the request\n    allows (CHANGE-G2 item 2). Rollback keeps the G1 rule: the budget field is\n    validated for shape but never caps the emitted units, hence ``None``."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 69,
            "end": 69,
            "text": "Backward-compatible helper returning the validated budget."
          }
        ],
        "release.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 12,
            "text": "JSON transport for the release planner.\n\nImport direction: release.py -> planner.py only. This layer parses stdin, maps\na parse failure to ``invalid-json``, delegates every domain decision to\nplanner.plan (which owns environment/action/budget/graph semantics) and prints\none JSON object. Keeping policy out of here is what lets the domain error codes\npass through unchanged instead of being re-derived or masked.\n\nThe process must exit normally for every request, including domain errors, and\nmust not create files, read anything else or execute a deployment; only stdout\ncarries the result."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 23,
            "end": 23,
            "text": "# ValueError covers JSONDecodeError for malformed or empty stdin; the"
          },
