      "terminalAnnotations": {
        "journal.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 10,
            "text": "Validate, coalesce and index release records at the input boundary.\n\nThis module owns identity and replay *shape*: it never sorts, budgets or decides\nwhich actions run. The planner consumes its output and owns graph/order.\n\nReplay accounting note: ``applied`` is the caller's persisted release journal,\nnot state held by this service. Every request replays it from scratch, so this\nmodule must be able to answer \"which changes are already done\" without touching\nany external store."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 18,
            "end": 18,
            "text": "# bool is a subclass of int in Python; SPEC says integers exclude booleans,"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 19,
            "end": 19,
            "text": "# so a strict type check is required rather than isinstance()."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 29,
            "end": 29,
            "text": "# Dependencies are an unordered set: order is irrelevant and repeated"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 30,
            "end": 30,
            "text": "# entries carry no extra meaning. A self-reference is rejected here even"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 31,
            "end": 31,
            "text": "# though it would also be caught as a cycle later: it is a record-shape"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 32,
            "end": 32,
            "text": "# bug, and SPEC gives ``invalid-change`` precedence over graph errors."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 42,
            "end": 42,
            "text": "Return normalized records: dicts with id/component/units/depends_on(tuple)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 61,
            "end": 61,
            "text": "Return the persisted journal as an ordered, de-duplicated tuple of IDs."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 64,
            "end": 64,
            "text": "# A bare string is iterable, so ``\"ab\"`` would silently become ``[\"a\",\"b\"]``"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 65,
            "end": 65,
            "text": "# and an out-of-type journal could approve changes that were never applied."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 66,
            "end": 66,
            "text": "# Type-check the container before iterating it."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 81,
            "end": 87,
            "text": "Collapse identical duplicate records; reject same-ID conflicting records.\n\n    Identity is the *whole record* (id, component, units and dependency set),\n    not just the id. Two records sharing an id but differing anywhere else mean\n    the upstream data is inconsistent; silently keeping the last one would\n    change what gets deployed, so we fail with ``conflicting-change`` instead."
          }
        ],
        "planner.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 5,
            "text": "Compose policy, journal data and the dependency graph into a plan.\n\nDependency direction: this module may import policy and journal; neither may\nimport this module. Transport (release.py) only imports ``plan``."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 13,
            "end": 18,
            "text": "The one place an error envelope is built.\n\n    Every failure path returns this complete object (empty actions, zero\n    units); callers must never observe a partially built plan, which is the\n    \"disallowed operation emits no partial actions\" rule from SPEC."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 23,
            "end": 29,
            "text": "Reject unknown dependency targets and unknown applied IDs.\n\n    Checked before cycle detection on purpose: a graph that is both dangling and\n    cyclic reports ``unknown-dependency`` (the first SPEC-ordered fault), and\n    rollback needs every applied ID to exist in the graph before it can be\n    replayed. Cycles are detected separately in ``_topological``."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 40,
            "end": 51,
            "text": "Return the full deterministic topological order of every change.\n\n    Tie-breaking is lexicographic smallest ID among *currently eligible*\n    changes (deps satisfied), implemented with a min-heap. Sorting ``depends_on``\n    in journal and using a heap here makes the order independent of input order\n    and of dict/hash iteration, so it is reproducible across processes.\n\n    Ordering is computed over the *full* graph, applied nodes included. That is\n    what lets ``plan`` filter applied entries (their dependents still see their\n    dependencies satisfied) and ``rollback`` walk the reverse of one identical\n    order rather than a second, possibly different, ordering."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 69,
            "end": 69,
            "text": "# Fewer emitted nodes than changes means at least one cycle. We raise"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 70,
            "end": 70,
            "text": "# instead of returning the partial order: a partial order is exactly the"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 71,
            "end": 71,
            "text": "# \"partial actions\" that must never reach the caller."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 92,
            "end": 92,
            "text": "# Rollback replays the applied journal in reverse dependency order."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 93,
            "end": 93,
            "text": "# Budget was validated above but intentionally does NOT cap rollback:"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 94,
            "end": 94,
            "text": "# stopping an unroll midway is worse than overrunning a plan budget."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 97,
            "end": 97,
            "text": "# Already applied changes satisfy dependencies (order was computed on"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 98,
            "end": 98,
            "text": "# the full graph) but must not be re-emitted, so they consume no new"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 99,
            "end": 99,
            "text": "# budget. Accounting therefore happens on exactly the emitted set."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 103,
            "end": 103,
            "text": "# Budget is checked after replay/dedup so duplicates are charged once."
          }
        ],
        "policy.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 6,
            "text": "Validate release policy before computing actions.\n\nPolicy answers only *is this request allowed at all*: environment, action and\nbudget legitimacy. It deliberately knows nothing about the dependency graph or\nwhich changes are already applied, so it cannot disagree with the planner."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 22,
            "end": 22,
            "text": "# Strict int test: ``True``/``False`` are ints in Python but SPEC excludes"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 23,
            "end": 23,
            "text": "# booleans, and a missing budget must not silently become 0."
          }
        ],
        "release.py": [
