      "terminalAnnotations": {
        "journal.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 10,
            "text": "Normalize release records at the input boundary.\n\nThis module owns input validation, record identity and replay state. It runs\nbefore graph construction in planner.py, so planner.py never sees a malformed\nrecord, a duplicated ID, or a non-array depends_on/applied value.\n\nImport direction: journal.py imports nothing from the other modules. It must\nstay a leaf because planner.py imports it; importing planner back would create\na cycle."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 18,
            "end": 24,
            "text": "Validate a JSON array of change IDs.\n\n    depends_on and applied share this shape: a JSON array of strings. Anything\n    else (a bare string, null, numbers, mixed element types) is malformed input\n    rather than a graph problem, so it fails here as invalid-change instead of\n    surfacing later as unknown-dependency."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 34,
            "end": 40,
            "text": "Validate `changes` and collapse identical duplicate records.\n\n    Append-only release records can be replayed, so duplicates are expected.\n    Equality covers id, component, units and the dependency *set*, because the\n    planner builds a graph where dependency order carries no meaning. The first\n    occurrence is kept so the result does not depend on which copy arrived last."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 48,
            "end": 48,
            "text": "# type() rather than isinstance() so that booleans, which subclass int,"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 49,
            "end": 49,
            "text": "# are rejected as units."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 62,
            "end": 62,
            "text": "# A repeated ID is either an exact replay or a competing definition."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 63,
            "end": 63,
            "text": "# Merging conflicting definitions would silently pick one, so the"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 64,
            "end": 64,
            "text": "# planner must fail closed instead of guessing."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 74,
            "end": 79,
            "text": "Read the caller-supplied persisted release journal.\n\n    `applied` defaults to empty. Only the shape is checked here; whether each ID\n    names a known change is decided in planner.py, which holds the full graph.\n    Duplicates are harmless because the value is consumed as a set."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 84,
            "end": 100,
            "text": "Validate the optional rollback `retain` list. G2-3.\n\n    `retain` names applied change IDs that must survive a rollback (default\n    empty). It is checked here rather than in planner.py because it is a shape\n    question about caller input, the same class of check as depends_on/applied;\n    the planner only needs the resulting set to compute the dependency closure.\n\n    Deliberately *not* routed through _id_list(): that helper reports\n    invalid-change, which would make a bad retain indistinguishable from a bad\n    `changes` record. `retain` is checked *after* `applied` is read so every\n    element can be required to be already applied — a retained ID that was never\n    released is a caller error, not an empty rollback. Duplicates are harmless\n    since the value is consumed as a set.\n\n    Only rollback calls this; a plan carrying `retain` is unaffected because the\n    key is never read for that action."
          }
        ],
        "planner.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 7,
            "text": "Compose policy and journal data into a plan.\n\nImport direction: planner.py imports journal.py and policy.py and is imported by\nrelease.py. Both of its dependencies are leaves, so no cycle can form. This\nmodule owns graph construction, deterministic ordering, retain pruning and\nresult assembly; it never touches stdin/stdout."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 15,
            "end": 19,
            "text": "The single error shape. Every failure carries no partial actions.\n\n    A disallowed operation must not leak a partial plan, so actions/units are\n    fixed here rather than at each raise site."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 24,
            "end": 34,
            "text": "Return the full deterministic topological order of every change.\n\n    The order covers all changes, including already-applied ones, because\n    rollback needs to walk applied changes in reverse of their original\n    execution order. Replay state only affects which subset is emitted later.\n\n    Kahn's algorithm with a heap over currently eligible IDs gives lexicographic\n    tie-breaking among eligible nodes. It is iterative, so the cycle case that\n    a recursive DFS would blow the stack on (a long cycle) terminates by simply\n    leaving nodes unemitted."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 42,
            "end": 42,
            "text": "# The persisted journal referencing an ID absent from `changes` means the"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 43,
            "end": 43,
            "text": "# caller's two inputs disagree; guessing would emit an unrunnable plan."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 64,
            "end": 64,
            "text": "# A cycle blocked the drain. This check runs on the full graph, so a cycle"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 65,
            "end": 65,
            "text": "# masked by `applied` (every member already released) still fails rather"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 66,
            "end": 66,
            "text": "# than reporting success on an unsatisfiable graph."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 72,
            "end": 83,
            "text": "Applied IDs kept because a retained ID needs them. G2-3.\n\n    Returns the retained IDs plus every dependency reachable from them that is\n    already applied. The walk follows `depends_on` in the release direction\n    (dependencies of dependencies), answering \"what must still be present for\n    these retained changes to keep working\".\n\n    Only applied nodes are added: an unapplied dependency is not part of the\n    current release and has no effect to preserve. The graph is acyclic by the\n    time this runs (_ordered already rejected cycles), but `keep` is still\n    tracked so a diamond dependency is visited once."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 99,
            "end": 106,
            "text": "Validate the request, then assemble the success or failure object.\n\n    Check order is part of the contract (G2-1): top-level shape → environment\n    validity → production freeze → action → budget → changes/applied → graph.\n    Because limit_for() stops at the freeze, a production request never reaches\n    the change or graph validation below, so a malformed graph there still\n    yields production-frozen rather than invalid-change / dependency-cycle."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 114,
            "end": 114,
            "text": "# Read after `applied` because a retain element must already be applied,"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 115,
            "end": 115,
            "text": "# and only for rollback so a plan ignores the key entirely (G2-3)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 123,
            "end": 123,
            "text": "# Only applied changes exist to undo, in reverse execution order. Budget"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 124,
            "end": 124,
            "text": "# was validated above but intentionally does not cap the result, and"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 125,
            "end": 125,
            "text": "# G2-3 keeps that: retain narrows the set, it never defers the remainder."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 130,
            "end": 130,
            "text": "# Already applied changes satisfy dependencies and spend no new budget,"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 131,
            "end": 131,
            "text": "# so they are filtered before the ceiling is checked."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 135,
            "end": 135,
            "text": "# A plan ceiling is the smaller of the request budget and the staging quota"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 136,
            "end": 136,
            "text": "# (G2-2). Rollback is validated for soundness only, so allows() is false"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 137,
            "end": 137,
            "text": "# there and the cap comparison is skipped rather than clamped."
          }
        ],
        "policy.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 7,
            "text": "Validate release policy before computing actions.\n\nPolicy owns three decisions only: whether the requested environment/action pair\nis permitted, how the budget constrains the result, and (G2-2) the staging plan\nquota. It is a leaf module: it\nimports nothing, so planner.py can depend on it without a cycle."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 9,
            "end": 9,
            "text": "# G2-1 makes this release staging-only. Production is frozen for *both*"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 10,
            "end": 10,
            "text": "# actions, so its empty set is the fail-closed representation: nothing is"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 11,
            "end": 11,
            "text": "# allowed there, before any actions can be emitted. The freeze decision itself"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 12,
            "end": 12,
            "text": "# is made by is_frozen() ahead of every other check; the entry exists so the"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 13,
            "end": 13,
            "text": "# table still answers \"what is permitted here\" for every valid environment."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 19,
            "end": 19,
            "text": "# G2-1: environments that exist but are eligible to do no work at all."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 22,
            "end": 22,
            "text": "# G2-2: effective ceiling for a staging plan. The request budget may be larger,"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 23,
            "end": 23,
            "text": "# but a plan may never emit more than this many units."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 32,
            "end": 36,
            "text": "Whether an already-validated `environment` accepts any action. G2-1.\n\n    Takes the value validated by limit_for() so the freeze decision can never be\n    made on an unvalidated string."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 41,
            "end": 48,
            "text": "The ceiling a plan is measured against. G2-2.\n\n    A staging plan may emit at most min(request budget, STAGING_PLAN_QUOTA)\n    units, so a request that permits more than the quota is still refused when\n    the emitted total exceeds it. Rollback is validated but never capped, so its\n    request budget is returned unchanged; allows() decides whether the caller\n    applies the ceiling at all."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 55,
            "end": 66,
            "text": "Validate environment, action and budget; return the request budget.\n\n    Ordering is load-bearing for G2-1: an unknown/absent environment is\n    malformed input and keeps its G1 code, invalid-environment. The caller then\n    calls is_frozen() *before* check_action() and before any graph or change\n    validation, so production reports production-frozen even when the rest of\n    the request is broken.\n\n    The budget is validated for every action, but the ceiling is applied only by\n    plan. Rollback walks a persisted journal, so capping it would leave the\n    environment half-reverted; see allows() below."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 71,
            "end": 71,
            "text": "# G2-1: refuse here, before action and budget are inspected, so the"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 72,
            "end": 72,
            "text": "# freeze cannot be pre-empted by unsupported-action or invalid-budget."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 78,
            "end": 78,
            "text": "# type() rather than isinstance() so booleans never count as a budget."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 85,
            "end": 90,
            "text": "Whether the budget caps the result for this action.\n\n    Only plan is capped. Rollback must be able to undo the whole applied set\n    regardless of budget, otherwise the budget could block recovery. G2-3 keeps\n    this rule after retain pruning: retained IDs are skipped, never deferred."
          }
        ],
