      "terminalAnnotations": {
        "journal.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 7,
            "text": "Normalize release records at the input boundary.\n\nThis module owns everything that is about *records and identity*: shape validation\nof `changes` and of the caller-supplied `applied` journal, duplicate collapsing, and\nthe check that every referenced ID exists. It deliberately knows nothing about\nordering, budgets or environments -- those belong to planner/policy."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 15,
            "end": 19,
            "text": "Validate `changes` and return one normalized record per distinct ID.\n\n    Dedup happens before accounting and graph construction (SPEC G1), so callers can\n    rely on each returned id appearing exactly once."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 29,
            "end": 29,
            "text": "# Identity is resolved here, before any graph reference is checked, so a"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 30,
            "end": 30,
            "text": "# malformed pair of records always reports conflicting-change rather than"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 31,
            "end": 31,
            "text": "# whatever dependency error the first copy might also have."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 33,
            "end": 33,
            "text": "# Equal duplicates are collapsed silently: they are the same operation, and"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 34,
            "end": 34,
            "text": "# charging them twice would make budget checks depend on input repetition."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 44,
            "end": 44,
            "text": "# Integers exclude booleans: `type(...) is int` rejects True/False, which"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 45,
            "end": 45,
            "text": "# isinstance(True, int) would accept."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 60,
            "end": 60,
            "text": "# Order and repetition are not part of a change's identity, so dependencies"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 61,
            "end": 61,
            "text": "# are stored sorted and de-duplicated. This makes equality below a plain"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 62,
            "end": 62,
            "text": "# comparison and keeps the graph edges deterministic."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 68,
            "end": 68,
            "text": "Equality covers id, component, units and the *set* of dependencies."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 75,
            "end": 80,
            "text": "Validate the persisted release journal and return it as a set of IDs.\n\n    The journal is a membership record, not a sequence: repeated IDs carry no extra\n    meaning, and it never contributes units or budget. Only shape is checked here;\n    the unknown-ID check needs the change list and lives in `resolve`."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 90,
            "end": 94,
            "text": "Check that the graph and the journal only reference known change IDs.\n\n    Missing graph targets and unknown applied IDs both mean the caller's persisted\n    state disagrees with the request, which is reported as unknown-dependency."
          }
        ],
        "planner.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 1,
            "end": 5,
            "text": "Compose policy and journal data into a plan.\n\nOwns graph construction, deterministic ordering, replay (already-applied changes),\nbudget enforcement, rollback order, and the success/failure payloads."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 20,
            "end": 20,
            "text": "# Order of checks is observable and is part of the contract:"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 21,
            "end": 21,
            "text": "# environment/action, budget shape, change shape, applied shape, identity"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 22,
            "end": 22,
            "text": "# conflicts, unknown IDs, cycles -- and only then the budget magnitude."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 31,
            "end": 31,
            "text": "# Budget counts only emitted units, after replay and dedup, so already"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 32,
            "end": 32,
            "text": "# applied entries free budget even though they remain in the graph."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 36,
            "end": 36,
            "text": "# Rollback walks the *full* order in reverse and keeps applied entries, so"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 37,
            "end": 37,
            "text": "# it undoes in the exact inverse of a complete plan regardless of what is"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 38,
            "end": 38,
            "text": "# applied now; the budget is validated but not capped (SPEC G1)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 47,
            "end": 51,
            "text": "Deterministic topological order with lexicographic ID tie-breaking.\n\n    The successor graph is built over *all* changes, applied or not, so a cycle or a\n    dangling reference is detected even when the emitted action list would be empty."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 56,
            "end": 56,
            "text": "# target is the prerequisite, emitted before change."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 60,
            "end": 60,
            "text": "# Eligible nodes are kept in a min-heap so the lexicographically smallest ID is"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 61,
            "end": 61,
            "text": "# always taken next. NOTE: this is *not* the same as sorting by ID up front; the"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 62,
            "end": 62,
            "text": "# eligible set only grows as nodes are emitted. Example: \"b\", \"m\" (depends on \"z\")"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 63,
            "end": 63,
            "text": "# and \"z\". Ordering the IDs first would emit b, m, (blocked) z; the rule here"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 64,
            "end": 64,
            "text": "# emits b, z, m because \"m\" is not eligible until \"z\" has been emitted. Plain"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 65,
            "end": 65,
            "text": "# string comparison is used (uppercase sorts before lowercase), so the order is"
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 66,
            "end": 66,
            "text": "# reproducible across runs and Python versions."
          },
          {
            "kind": "comment",
            "start": 78,
            "end": 78,
            "text": "# Anything left has a non-zero indegree forever: a cycle (self-loops included)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
            "start": 84,
            "end": 88,
            "text": "Unapplied changes in topological order.\n\n    An applied entry still counts as a satisfied dependency (it is in the graph), it\n    is just not emitted and does not consume budget."
          }
        ],
        "policy.py": [
          {
            "kind": "docstring",
